# Scope, Deliverables and Budgetary Pricing

## Scope of Work

Johnston Engineering will provide mechanical engineering design, software control system design, mechanical engineering analysis, component fabrication and procurement, commissioning, and delivery of a completed, containerized system that meets the specified performance criteria described in the “Capture6 Project Tortuga RFP” and “Project Tortuga – Process Design Details” documents provided by Capture6. 

Johnston Engineering is targeting fitting the four subsystems into two 40’ shipping containers connected by piping, power and communications cabling.  

## Deliverables

### System Integration

- **Specification of OTS Equipment and Hardware:** Define all required components for system integration.

- **Design of custom components:**** **Custom mechanical hardware and component design, system layout, and packaging for containerized system.

- **Procurement:**

- Key equipment procurement with assistance from Capture6.

- Procurement of all necessary components for system fabrication.

- **Fabrication and Commissioning:** Fabricate and commission the system.

- **System Testing:** Conduct testing to meet performance criteria developed by Capture6.

- **Documentation:** Produce comprehensive documentation to convey all engineering work and learnings to Capture6 and potential third-party operators.

- **Optional Operation Support:** Provide housing and operation support for the system between deployments, including maintenance, modification, and testing to ensure continuous process improvement.

### Comprehensive Documentation

- **Operating Manual/Instructions:** Including procedures for interconnections, startup, and shutdown.

- **Piping and Instrumentation Diagrams (P****&****IDs):** Detailed diagrams of the system's piping and instrumentation.

- **Layout Drawings:** Complete layout drawings of the system.

- **Bill of Materials:** A detailed bill of materials with components mapped to their installation locations.

- **Design Documentation:** Documentation of calculations and key design choices in a memo format.

- **CAD Models:** All CAD models used in the project in SolidWorks, STEP, or IGES format.

- **Technical Configuration Guide:** A guide outlining all components of the system and their configuration parameters.

## Budgetary Price Proposal 

Johnston Engineering estimates a total engineering labor cost of **$9****22****,000**** USD**** **to deliver the Capture6 containerized demonstration system. Johnston Engineering proposes a time and materials (T&M) arrangement. Labor is billed at a rate of $195 per hour, and subcontractors and hardware purchased are billed on a cost-plus structure of cost +25%.  Our pricing assumptions for task level of complexity drive our labor pricing and we are very open to guidance from Capture6 to accomplish tasks easier, faster and for less cost. Detailed costing information is outlined below.

Please note, this proposal does not include hardware costs. Material and equipment expenses have not been estimated at this stage. Johnston Engineering will provide a detailed costing of materials and quote options for Capture6 approval once the project progresses further into the detailed design phase. 

### Equipment 

Equipment in general has not been quoted, but Johnston Engineering would like to share pricing obtained on a few key items.  Equipment prices include markup, but not tax or shipping. 

- Tanks: 

Johnston Engineering was unable to obtain pricing for the requested tank material (concrete, rubber lined concrete, glass reinforced epoxy) to meet the proposal submission deadline.  We have received order of magnitude costing on polymer tanks (polypropylene and polyethylene) that show good compatibility with NaCl, NAH, HCL, NaCO3.  If alternative materials are approved by Capture6, these tanks provide a less expensive and lighter option.  Johnston Engineering calculated tank size based on provided flow rate, 4 hours batch time, 80% tank fill as shown in Appendix B.  We received a polymer tank **budgetary cost: $3****6****,00****0** excluding the rectangular T-0301 under the DAC unit which could not be quoted in time to meet proposal deadline. 

- CSTR: 

Johnston Engineering was unable to obtain pricing for the requested CSTR material (Teflon lined steel) to meet the proposal submission deadline.  We have received order of magnitude costing on a polymer CSTR (High Density polyethylene) that show good compatibility with HCL and NaCO3.  If alternative materials are approved by Capture6, a polymer CSTR provides a less expensive and lighter option. Polymer tank and polymer mixer: **budgetary cost: $1****5****,****5****00.**  

- UL508 Panel 

Two UL508 panels are assumed necessary to house the Programmable Logic Controller, switching and cabling and one panel is needed for each of the two containers. Each panel cost is estimated at $6,500 for a total **budgetary ****cost for the two panels of $13,000**

### Engineering, fabrication and verification testing labor

Engineering, fabrication and verification testing labor cost is estimated at $922,000 and is summarized in the table below.  The largest single category is mechanical design and requires 109 person-days at $195/hour.   The second largest category is mechanical engineering requiring 145 person-days at $195/hour.  The largest mechanical design and engineering tasks are design and structural analysis of structural frames to house and support tanks and equipment. Our assumption is that these frames must withstand transportation acceleration loads and structural Finite Element Analysis (FEA) will be required.  

Program management is calculated at 5% of mechanical engineering which is historically accurate at Johnston Engineering.   These three categories represent 85% of project labor cost and will be completed by Johnston Engineering.   

Electrical engineering, software engineering, and electrical technician & electrical design work will be completed by subcontractor InfoTech NorthStar.  These electrical and software tasks require 74.5 person-days of work at $200/hour and 13.75 person-days of work at $103.50/hour and subcontractor labor prices are shown marked up and represent the cost to Capture6.  

This table also shows a recommended safety factor of 20% which has been shown historically accurate to cover cost over-runs at Johnston Engineering assuming no increase in project scope.   Detailed project labor pricing categories and assumptions are listed in Appendix A and calculations to size tanks are listed in Appendix B.  

The optional task to "house and operate the System for periods between deployments to project locations, providing part-time technician support for maintenance, modification, and testing, in collaboration with the Capture6 Engineering Team" to be a good fit, and we are interested in providing these services. We have the resources and space to support housing 1-2 full units of 2x containers each and will coordinate storage as necessary for additional units. Our facility includes two buildings, one of which is a 5,000 sq ft shop with an adjacent large, gated parking lot which we have used for a 40ft ISO containerized project. We will support this effort at our standard T&M rate and provide a detailed quote at a later date as details emerge. The cost of this option is not included in the pricing provided in this proposal. 

### Pricing and Billing Process

### Pricing

- This proposal is based on a time and material (T&M) hourly rate of $195 for time and a 25% markup for materials. Labor is billed at a rate of $195 per hour, hardware purchased, and subcontractors are billed on a cost-plus structure of cost +25%. Material and equipment expenses have not been estimated at this stage due to the labor-intensive nature of costing. Johnston Engineering will provide a detailed costing of materials and quote options for Capture6 approval once the project progresses further into the detailed design phase

### Billing

- Johnston Engineering works from a retainer payment agreement. All services are pre-paid in monthly reserved hours. Unused hours do not expire and will roll over to the following month. At every invoice cycle, Johnston Engineering provides Capture6 with its current positive or negative balance and makes recommendations for future hourly purchases. All invoices are due on receipt. Upon project completion, any remaining hours can be used for future Capture6 projects or refunded at the sole discretion of Capture6.

### Project Management 

The project will be led personally by Johnston Engineering President, Andy Johnston, P.E., with support from a dedicated team of 1-3 mechanical engineers and 1-3 mechanical designers depending on the desired project completion speed. 

Johnston Engineering intends to use our subcontractor, InfoTech North Star (ITNS) for electrical and software engineering. Johnston Engineering will handle all mechanical engineering, procurement, and commissioning. tasks outlined in the budgetary cost proposal and manage the overall project. ITNS will be responsible for all software engineering tasks, including software development and system controls, as detailed in the budgetary cost proposal. Johnston Engineering will manage the team and maintain regular communication with Capture6 through 1-2 primary points of contact.

At Johnston Engineering, we pride ourselves on a structured and transparent project management approach. Our methodology ensures that the project remains on schedule and within budget, with clear and consistent communication throughout, which is further outlined below:

- **Schedule and Budget Management**

- We manage the team to adhere to the agreed-upon schedule and budget. Regular check-ins are conducted weekly or biweekly via video meetings to maintain alignment and address any issues promptly.

- A conservative project schedule has been created based on the tasks and task durations identified in Appendix A.  Johnston Engineering has no insight to the level of urgency required by Capture6, so modestly paced project schedule has been presented.  Schedule assumptions include the following: Task durations in Appendix A and in section 2.3.2 have been multiplied by 1.5x those values shown and a single individual was assumed to accomplish work in each labor category (E.G. 1 mechanical engineer, 1 mechanical designer etc.)  The schedule can be easily compressed and likely halved by adding additional staff to the project and improving serialization of tasks with the penalty of a small increase in team duplicative labor such as increasing head count in meetings.  This should be a topic of further discussion to better understand Capture6’s needs and desires.    

- The complete project duration is 514 days assuming one staff per labor category.  By far, the largest task is engineering design at 308 days which covers early communications tasks through Critical Design Review (CDR) and hardware ordering.  The next largest task is fabrication at 165 days which has been very conservatively scheduled as serial tasks with two individuals building the hardware. Testing and validation is scheduled for 18 days which is likely the only task category that can easily take longer than scheduled as unforeseen problems tend to arise during testing. Project close out and documentation is scheduled for 22 days where deliverable documents identified in section 2.2.1 are generated. 

- **Communication: **

- Action items and updates are reviewed in team meetings and communicated via email between meetings to ensure accountability, continuous progress, and clarity. We maintain a rolling meeting PowerPoint presentation that records all current and past engineering progress, notes, questions, budget, action items, and schedule updates providing a comprehensive project timeline and reference that is sent to Capture6 at the end of each team meeting.

- **Billing Transparency: **

- Our billing process is broken down into detailed categories allowing Capture6 to see exactly how much time was spent on each task. For each labor charge, invoices will detail the date, labor category, description of work performed, and hours used. For materials charges, invoices will detail the date, description of the hardware, and cost. 

### Intellectual Property (IP) Rights

Johnston Engineering will assign all intellectual property related to this project to Capture6. Johnston Engineering is willing to assist Capture6 and patent attorneys with IP-related communications such as in transferring IP, generating patent figures, etc. We have not budgeted any IP transfer time in this proposal. 

# Proposal Conclusion: 

Johnston Engineering is committed to delivering exceptional engineering services and high-quality results for Capture6. Our engineering capabilities, combined with our past project experience ensure that we are well-suited to meet the deliverables of this project. We believe our unique qualifications and project experience make Johnston Engineering an excellent fit for this project in multiple ways. We have designed and built complete carbon capture pilot plants in ISO containers in 2024.  We are currently working on other extremely relevant projects including green technologies packaged in ISO containers and electrolytic processes including a ground up design for a hydrogen electrolyzer, a carbon dioxide to formic acid electrolytic process, and green energy storage using a carbon dioxide process. 

At Johnston Engineering, we specialize in first-of-a-kind systems and have experience working through all project phases, from initial concept sketches to prototypes, scale-ups, and pilot plants. We are familiar with navigating the complexities of new systems to make them a reality and function as intended. 

Johnston Engineering and InfoTech NorthStar have served clients both nationally and internationally in Washington, California, Colorado, New York, Florida, Alaska, Canada and Mexico. and We look forward to the opportunity to collaborate with Capture6 and contribute to the success of your demonstration and larger carbon capture initiatives.

Perhaps most importantly, we specifically focus on advancing clean technologies. Our research and development expertise positions us uniquely to work across various emerging markets, but we have actively chosen to focus on clean energy systems to contribute to a better world. Over 80% of our 2023 revenue was in clean technologies and it will be a greater percentage in 2024. Our objective is to play our part by reducing technical risk for our clients, enabling them to achieve business success and drive meaningful change for a cleaner world.  This is what gets us up early every day and keeps us working late.  I can tell you very honestly, we share a common mission, and we very much want to work for you and see your success.  

